# main + production (ff-only only)

`production` is **promote-only**. It must always be a fast-forward of `main` (same tip, or `main` ahead and then ff). Never author commits on `production`. Never create `Merge main: …` commits.

This divergence happened before because agents used non-ff merges onto `production`. That is forbidden.

## Hard rules

1. **Never** `git commit` on `production`.
2. **Never** `git merge origin/main` without `--ff-only`.
3. **Never** `git merge --no-ff`, rebase onto production, or cherry-pick onto production as the normal path.
4. Work lands on **`main`** (or a PR into `main`). Package publishes from **`main`**.
5. Railway docs / example-store / status / Shopify deploy from **`production` only** (never point them at `main`). See `DEPLOY.md`.
6. After shippable work on `main` that docs/Railway should show, promote `production` in the same session unless the user says main-only.

## Promote (only allowed form)

```bash
git fetch origin
git checkout production
git merge --ff-only origin/main
git push origin production
git checkout main
```

If `--ff-only` fails, **stop**. Do not invent a merge commit. Tell the user branches diverged; recover with an explicit backup + `reset --hard` to `main` (force-with-lease) only with user approval.

## After promote

Confirm:

```bash
test "$(git rev-parse origin/main)" = "$(git rev-parse origin/production)" \
  || git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/production origin/main
```

`origin/production` must equal `origin/main`, or be a strict ancestor of `origin/main` only momentarily before the next ff promote catches up (prefer equal tips after promote).
